/* auto-actions.js - Auto Actions: Smart Actions that switch on by themselves
 * when the Core sees your weapon (D122). window.ZSAUTO, and the editor's
 * `auto` and `autogun` screens.
 *
 * ES5 ONLY, like every file phone.html loads (docs/DESIGN.md): no arrow
 * function, template literal, let/const or class. Everything that reaches
 * innerHTML goes through esc().
 *
 * -- What the owner asked for ----------------------------------------------
 *
 * "This tab has Smart Actions but activated by certain parameters detected
 * from CV that I will train. Let's start with Call of Duty: a list of AR,
 * SMG, LMG, pistols; you click one and it expands and shows the gun and the
 * real logo of the gun in game, and you can make Smart Actions for each gun,
 * and then I will wire in the parameters and detection for the P4 to detect
 * when that gun is equipped, and it will activate whatever Smart Action the
 * user has for that gun." The Smart Actions tab stays exactly as it is.
 *
 * -- How it is built, and why nothing here is a second anything --------------
 *
 * THE LIST is the game's weapon catalog, one JSON file per game pack in
 * game-data/<pack id>/catalog.json (the pack decides the game, D115). It is
 * fetched the way game-packs.js fetches packs: an app file, once, cached.
 *
 * A WEAPON'S SMART ACTIONS are ordinary Smart Actions in the config's
 * accessibilityActions, each carrying an `auto` object that names its weapon:
 *
 *     { id, name, trigger, steps, ..., enabled: false,
 *       auto: { pack: "cod-warzone", item: "assault_rifles.m15_mod_0", on: true } }
 *
 * `enabled` is ALWAYS false in the document, and that is the whole safety
 * story in one field: D110's inert default is "the profile's own flag
 * stands", so an Auto Action stored switched on would run on every gun on a
 * Core that has not read the screen yet, and on every Core with no capture
 * board, for ever. The player's own on/off switch for one of them is
 * `auto.on`. They are edited in smart-actions.js's own room, scoped to the
 * weapon (ZS_SMART.renderScoped): the same editor, the same Ready-made
 * library, never a second one. The Smart Actions tab never lists them.
 *
 * A TRAINED WEAPON is a taught picture (D110) in a watched `match` area, the
 * 32x16 greyscale patch the Core compares, carrying the same catalog id and,
 * in `actions`, the ids of that weapon's Auto Actions whose `auto.on` is not
 * false:
 *
 *     vision.regions[k].match.templates[j] =
 *       { label: "M15 Mod 0", item: "assault_rifles.m15_mod_0",
 *         patch: "<512 bytes, base64>", actions: ["zs-..."] }
 *
 * That is D110's own tie, unchanged, so the wire needed nothing new: each
 * Auto Action becomes an ACTIONS record, each trained weapon a VISION
 * picture whose mask names its actions, and the device's existing rule
 * (D110 section 4, D119) turns them on while the weapon stands and off on
 * any other weapon or on "nothing I recognise". retie() below is the one
 * place the tie is computed, and it runs inside every write this tab makes.
 *
 * AN UNTRAINED WEAPON'S ACTIONS NEVER RUN, in three places: the document
 * stores them off; both encoders (zs-profile-encode.js, encode.py) drop an
 * `auto` action no taught picture names and send a named one with its
 * enabled bit clear, whatever the document says; and so the Core never
 * receives them at all (host suite `vision`, "Auto Actions (D122)").
 *
 * PICTURES STAY IN THE APP (D120). The catalog's weapon pictures are app
 * files. The owner's own crop of a taught weapon, the thing the Core looks
 * for, is kept in zs-photos.js's store under a drawer of its own and never
 * sent. The only picture-shaped bytes that reach the Core are the 32x16
 * patches D110 already carries, because they are detection data.
 *
 * -- A game where you are always somebody: operators, then their guns (D125) -
 *
 * The owner, on Rainbow Six Siege: "on Siege you are always an op, so lay it
 * out as: you choose your op, then that opens a tab of all of that op's
 * guns, then you can make a profile for each op basically, and the gun."
 *
 * A catalog can carry more than one KIND of thing (game-data/README.md, the
 * `kinds` form). The kind whose items have a loadout (or that the other
 * kind's `usedBy` names) is the LEAD: the room lists it, exactly as it lists
 * a single-kind game's weapons today. The other is CARRIED: it is reached
 * through a lead item's page. So Siege's room is the operators, Attackers
 * and Defenders; an operator's page is his own Smart Actions (the scoped
 * room, as a gun's page is) and his guns; and a gun opened there is scoped
 * to the PAIR:
 *
 *     own    { auto: { pack, item: "attackers.ash", on } }
 *     pair   { auto: { pack, item: "assault_rifles.r4_c", with: "attackers.ash", on } }
 *
 * An own action is tied to the operator's picture. A pair action is tied to
 * BOTH pictures, the operator's and the gun's, and only while both are taught
 * and live (retie), so it is on the wire only when the Core can see both. The
 * Core runs an action two areas name only while BOTH stand on a picture
 * naming it (zs_vision.c resolve(), D125): Ash's R4-C macro runs while Ash
 * and the R4-C are on screen together, never for Iana's R4-C and never on
 * Ash's pistol. A gun's picture is taught once and serves every operator who
 * carries it. A single-kind game (Warzone) has no `with` anywhere and every
 * rule above reduces to exactly D122's.
 *
 * "RECOGNISE GUNS TOO" is one switch per config, on by default. Off moves the
 * carried kind's pictures out of vision.regions into autoActions.parked
 * (park), so they are not in VISION, and the pair actions lose their ties
 * (retie), so the encoders drop them (D122's rule for an action no picture
 * names). The operators' own actions carry on. Nothing is deleted: on
 * (unpark) puts every area back where it was and retie ties the pairs again.
 * On the wire an ignored kind is indistinguishable from one never taught;
 * the parked copy rides only in the editable copy (the SOURCE sidecar).
 *
 * -- A game whose guns stand on their own too: modes, and All guns (D126) --
 *
 * The owner, on Fortnite: "add all the Fortnite modes and known guns and
 * everything, and a search bar for it". Fortnite is two kinds as Siege is:
 * its MODES lead (each mode's `loadout` is its pool of guns today) and its
 * GUNS are carried. What differs is that a Fortnite gun is worth actions of
 * its own, whatever the mode, because the Core need not know the mode for
 * the gun to be useful. So the carried kind may name an ALL entry
 * (`"all": "All guns"` on the kind, game-data/README.md): the room then
 * shows one fold at the top holding every item of that kind, vaulted ones
 * included, grouped by the kind's own groups, and an item opened there has
 * its OWN actions, single-area, exactly like a Warzone weapon's:
 *
 *     own    { auto: { pack, item: "shotguns.pump_shotgun", on } }
 *     pair   { auto: { pack, item: "shotguns.pump_shotgun", with: "battle_royale.zero_build", on } }
 *
 * The first runs whenever the Core sees the Pump Shotgun, in any mode; the
 * second only while it sees Zero Build AND the Pump Shotgun (D125's AND),
 * so it never runs until the owner has taught the Core that mode. Nothing on
 * the wire is new: an own action is D122's, a pair is D125's.
 *
 * A lead item's loadout may hold ANY named lists, drawn as sections in the
 * order written (Siege's primary then secondary); a loadout of ONE list
 * (Fortnite's `pool`) is drawn by the carried kind's groups instead, because
 * forty guns under one heading is a list nobody finds a gun in. A game with
 * an All entry has no "Recognise guns too" switch: its guns are the point,
 * and a mode the owner does not want watched is simply never taught.
 *
 * SEARCH finds an item by its name, by an `aliases` word the catalog gives it
 * ("pump", "SCAR"), by the short forms the names spell out ("AR" for Assault
 * Rifle, "SMG" for Submachine Gun), and a class by its own code ("SG" lists
 * every shotgun). The room's field searches the lead list and the All fold
 * at once; a lead item's page has its own field over its pool.
 *
 * -- One Smart Action for many guns, and for a rarity (D134) ----------------
 *
 * The owner: "make sure it is easy to copy and apply the same scripts and
 * Smart Actions to multiple guns, since there will be so many", and, of
 * Warzone: "give the option to have a different script for each rarity of
 * gun". docs/AUTO-ACTIONS-LINKING.md is the design, and the section numbers
 * below are its. Two optional fields ride in `auto`:
 *
 *     link   { items: [ids], classes: [group ids], every: true, except: [ids] }
 *     tiers  [catalog tier ids]            absent is any rarity
 *
 * LINK makes ONE action run on many guns. retie() names it from every
 * linked gun's taught picture, so the wire carries one ACTIONS record and a
 * mask bit per gun, which is D110's own tie: no firmware change. A class or
 * `every` is a RULE read against the catalog at each retie, so next
 * season's SMG joins "every SMG" by itself. The action still lives on one
 * gun (auto.item): that gun's page edits it, and every other linked gun's
 * page lists it under "Linked from other guns", with Unlink and Make a
 * separate copy. COPY makes independent actions, one per gun. Link is the
 * default wherever both are offered.
 *
 * TIERS make an action one rarity's. No Core reads rarity yet (D132's name
 * areas are not wired) and a picture has no rarity to read, so a tier action
 * is never tied through a picture: it is kept in the config, marked "Needs
 * rarity detection", and never sent (D122's rule for an untaught gun). A
 * gun's page shows one rarity at a time (the strip), and each rarity is its
 * own room of the Smart Actions editor, so what is made there is that
 * rarity's.
 *
 * An action with neither field is exactly what it was before: the pins in
 * tools/test-auto-links.js hold old configs to their bytes.
 */
